## Callback call-graph edges

For every callback identity resolved above, a real call-graph edge (ASYNC_CALLBACKS
A2) from the registration call site to the method that will actually run: for a
concrete class, the class that declares the callback method (may be a superclass —
ordinary JVM method resolution); for a lambda or method reference (DYNAMIC_LINKAGE
L0/L1), its own implementation method directly, already resolved once via
LambdaMetafactory, never through CHA. This is a "may run" edge, like a profile's
synthetic edge or CHA's own dispatch expansion: it does not claim the registration
call itself guarantees execution (the thread may never start, the task may be
rejected or cancelled), and it never invents a thread identity. "Gap" means the
resolved class doesn't resolve the callback method anywhere in the analyzed hierarchy
(normally only when a class on that chain is missing from the input, not evidence the
callback itself is unreachable) — a lambda/method reference identity never has a Gap
here, since nothing further needs resolving.
